"""Original community mental health, inclusion and recovery teaching."""
def course(c,m):
 return c("SWP-058","Community Mental Health Rights and Recovery","community-mental-health-rights-and-recovery","Community Mental\nHealth Rights and\nRecovery",
 "Prepare a fictional rights-based community support proposal with person-defined goals, supported choice, appropriate peer roles and a bounded review of inclusion and access.",[
m("Rights supported choice and participation","Translate person-centred rights principles into a bounded service-access recommendation.",[
"A rights-based community service treats a person as someone with preferences, relationships, abilities and entitlements rather than as a diagnosis or a problem to manage. WHO's community guidance describes person-centred approaches and community inclusion across different service arrangements. In a fictional access review, ask who sets the goals, how information is explained and whether the person can participate meaningfully. Do not assume that receiving mental-health support removes the ability to make choices, or that a worker may decide every aspect of a person's life because the worker intends to help. [S21, S1]",
"Supported decision-making helps a person understand and communicate choices through appropriate assistance. It differs from replacing the person's preferences with another person's decision. The kind of support needed may vary with the decision, communication needs and circumstances. An introductory learner does not determine legal capacity, create a universal consent rule or decide a real safeguarding exception. Applicable law and qualified professional procedures remain necessary. A fictional proposal can identify an accessible explanation, a chosen support role and an unresolved authority question without making a legal finding. [S21, S1]",
"Participation also concerns the service itself. People with lived experience should be able to influence a relevant design or review decision through suitable, accessible arrangements. Involvement should not depend on disclosing a diagnosis publicly or agreeing with staff. The 2025 policy introduction connects governance, workforce and social determinants, including housing, work and exclusion. This supports asking what institutional rule or resource affects access. It does not prove that a locally proposed programme complies with Nigerian law or that participation alone removes all barriers to quality care. [S22, S21]",
"A bounded rights recommendation describes the actual barrier, its effect on participation, the person's stated preference and the responsible decision-maker. Avoid promises about benefit eligibility, legal remedies or service availability that have not been verified. Use non-stigmatising language and distinguish a reported concern from a confirmed policy or legal conclusion. For coursework, analyse only the supplied fictional situation, with no private health records or interviews. Qualified review must consider safety, local law and organisational responsibilities before any real change is implemented or presented as an approved standard. [S1, S21, S22]"],
"Fictional case: a community centre requires every adult using a mental-health programme to obtain family approval for routine activities. An adult asks to choose a communication supporter instead. The learner examines the rule and appropriate decision authority without making a legal-capacity ruling.",
"Draft a rights/access note with the actual rule, its effect, the person's preference, an accessible decision-support option, an authority question and a proposed review route.",
"A useful note recognises agency and practical support without assuming a diagnosis removes choice. It separates rights-based service design from a legal determination or a promise of eligibility.",["S21","S1","S22"],[
("A diagnosis does not define all preferences or abilities.","A fictional centre assumes all adults receiving mental-health support must want the same activities. What should change?","Ask about individual goals and provide meaningful participation.",["Use one staff preference for every participant.","Treat a diagnosis as the person's entire identity.","Require public health disclosure to express a preference."],"Person-centred support recognises individual priorities and strengths."),
("Decision support differs from substituting a preference.","An adult requests help understanding options, but a supporter chooses without asking. What distinction matters?","Helping the person decide is different from replacing the person's choice.",["Any helper automatically acquires all decision authority.","Explanation is unnecessary once a supporter attends.","A worker's preferred outcome defines informed choice."],"Support should enable the person's participation within appropriate procedures."),
("An introductory course cannot decide legal capacity.","A learner is asked to declare a real person's legal decision-making capacity. What fits the course boundary?","Identify the need for qualified context-specific legal and professional procedures.",["Issue a legal ruling from a worksheet score.","Assume every mental-health diagnosis has the same legal effect.","Use staff convenience as the capacity test."],"The course develops service reasoning rather than legal decision authority."),
("Lived-experience participation can address service governance.","A fictional service plans an access-rule review. Which involvement is meaningful?","Accessible input that can affect the stated decision and receives an accountable response.",["Attendance after all decisions are final.","Mandatory public diagnosis disclosure.","Excluding anyone who questions the proposed rule."],"Meaningful participation involves influence and suitable conditions."),
("Rights-based guidance does not establish local legal compliance.","A proposal cites WHO guidance and labels itself legally compliant in Nigeria. What remains necessary?","Applicable local legal and professional review of the actual proposal.",["No further review because an international source is cited.","A universal eligibility guarantee.","A claim that rights language proves service capacity."],"International guidance is a benchmark rather than a local legal approval."),
]),
m("Personal recovery peers and community life","Distinguish person-defined recovery goals from clinical outcomes and define appropriate peer-support conditions.",[
"Personal recovery can concern meaning, agency, relationships and participation in ordinary life, including when difficulties continue. Clinical outcomes, such as symptoms assessed by qualified professionals, answer different questions. Neither perspective requires an introductory worker to promise a cure or dismiss health needs. Ask what the person wants to pursue and what support or environmental change could make that feasible. A fictional goal might involve returning to a valued activity or rebuilding a chosen connection, with the person's own definition of progress rather than a standard goal imposed on every participant. [S21, S1]",
"Peer support can offer connection through shared lived experience, but a peer role needs clear boundaries, training, supervision and organisational support. A peer supporter is not automatically a clinician, emergency responder or unpaid substitute for every service gap. People should understand the role and be able to choose whether it fits their needs. Support arrangements also need to consider the peer worker's wellbeing, workload and compensation. An introductory course cannot train or certify an independent peer specialist by asking someone to share a personal mental-health history. [S21, S1]",
"The 2026 digital peer-support review examines outpatient interventions with varied designs and populations. It reports limited long-term and safety information and does not establish that any online group is safe, effective or suitable for a particular Nigerian community. Digital access, privacy, moderation and escalation arrangements matter, as do alternatives for people who cannot or do not wish to participate online. Do not promise engagement or recovery because a platform has a peer-support label. A referral to a group requires appropriate verification and an informed choice rather than an automatic enrolment. [R30, S21]",
"A person-defined support plan connects the chosen goal with practical conditions, accepted roles and a way to revisit preferences. A request to join an activity does not authorise sharing clinical records with its organiser. Distinguish assistance offered from participation that actually becomes accessible, and both from clinical improvement. Where medication, acute distress or specialist needs arise, qualified services retain their roles; a recovery goal is not advice to stop treatment. Use the fictional exercise to explain choices and boundaries, without testing a real group, contacting service users or collecting evidence of another person's recovery. [S1, S21, R30]"],
"Fictional case: an adult wants to reconnect with a community music group and is offered an unverified online peer forum instead. A learner asks about the adult's goal, access preferences and suitable support rather than treating forum enrolment as recovery.",
"Write a goal/support note with person-defined progress, one environmental barrier, an appropriate peer-role condition, a digital-access/privacy question and a separate clinical boundary.",
"A defensible note supports ordinary-life goals and informed options. It does not equate a peer label, online enrolment or activity attendance with clinical recovery or guaranteed safety.",["S21","S1","R30"],[
("Personal recovery and clinical outcomes are distinct.","A fictional adult values renewed participation while some difficulties continue. What is an appropriate interpretation?","Person-defined progress may matter alongside separately assessed clinical needs.",["Progress is impossible unless all symptoms vanish.","Valued participation proves a clinical cure.","A recovery goal means treatment should stop."],"Different outcome domains should be recognised without substituting one for another."),
("Peer roles require support and boundaries.","A centre expects a peer worker to manage every clinical emergency without supervision. What needs correction?","The role, training, supervision and qualified escalation arrangements.",["Only the worker's willingness to share a personal story.","A rule that peers must work without compensation.","A claim that lived experience grants prescribing authority."],"Shared experience does not replace clinical competence or organisational duties."),
("Digital peer-support findings do not validate every forum.","An organiser calls an unmoderated online forum safe because a 2026 review studied digital peers. What is missing?","Appraisal of the actual forum, access, moderation, privacy and appropriate response arrangements.",["Only the forum's popularity.","A guarantee transferred from a pooled study label.","Automatic consent from all potential participants."],"Interventions and conditions vary; the review is not blanket approval."),
("A person-defined goal should guide support options.","An adult wants a community music connection but is automatically enrolled in an unrelated group. What should be revisited?","The person's goal, preferences and feasible suitable options.",["How to persuade everyone to accept the same activity.","Whether staff convenience overrides the goal.","How to label enrolment as completed recovery."],"Support should respond to the person's priorities rather than a predetermined service output."),
("Community participation does not require unrestricted health disclosure.","A fictional activity organiser requests a participant's full clinical file. What should a support outline consider?","The specific purpose, minimum necessary information and appropriate permissions/governance.",["Automatic sharing because the activity may help.","A public copy in the course worksheet.","A belief that recovery orientation removes privacy duties."],"Supporting participation does not remove information boundaries."),
]),
m("Inclusion resources and accountable review","Connect social barriers with feasible cross-service action and evidence-aware review.",[
"A person may want an activity or service but face housing insecurity, transport cost, exclusion, inaccessible communication or conflicting work demands. A rights-based community plan examines these conditions without interpreting every barrier as a personal lack of motivation. The 2025 WHO introduction links mental-health reform with social and structural determinants. Identify the actual decision and the role that can act on it: a clinic may address an appointment process while another agency controls transport or accommodation. Coordination should clarify responsibilities, with the person involved, rather than promise one worker can solve every condition. [S22, S21, S1]",
"Evidence can inform this analysis without settling a particular person's circumstances. The adult coordination scoping review maps varied roles and service arrangements; its exclusions and terminology limits mean it does not establish one universally effective model. The Ibadan IJSDS survey adds local provider perspectives with small-sample, single-site and reporting limitations. Neither supports deciding what every service user experiences or whether a specific Nigerian programme causes recovery. Use them to ask about actual availability, barriers and responsibilities, alongside the person's appropriately obtained views and current authorised service information. [R32, R35, S21]",
"A practical review distinguishes a referral offered, a suitable service accepted, access achieved and progress toward the person's goal. More contacts do not necessarily mean better continuity or inclusion. If a process was inaccessible, examine the system before labelling the person non-compliant. Feedback arrangements should be voluntary, accessible and safe, with no requirement for a recovery narrative or public diagnosis. Real evaluation involving sensitive information needs appropriate governance; fictional coursework can test the quality of the proposed question without collecting actual service data or attempting clinical outcome measurement. [S21, S1, R32]",
"A recommendation should identify an authorised owner, a resource condition, the next agreed contact and a review point. State what the evidence supports and what remains uncertain. Where an option is unavailable, make that gap visible and seek an appropriate alternative through qualified roles rather than record a successful referral by assumption. A recovery-oriented service is accountable for how it responds to barriers and preferences; it should not pressure the person to endorse the programme's success story. Independent lived-experience, clinical, legal and accessibility review is still needed before this draft is approved for use. [S22, S21, S1]"],
"Fictional case: a service records five referral offers as successful community inclusion, although an adult cannot reach the venue. A learner separates the output from access, identifies the transport decision-maker and proposes an agreed review of the person's actual goal.",
"Draft an inclusion review with the goal, barrier, source/evidence limit, accountable role, resource condition, access question and unsupported claim to remove.",
"A sound review examines the actual barrier and feasible authority. It differentiates offers, access and personal progress, keeps evidence limits visible and avoids blaming the person for a system gap.",["S22","S21","S1","R32","R35"],[
("Social barriers should not be reduced to motivation labels.","A fictional adult misses a community appointment because transport is unaffordable. What should the review examine?","The access barrier and the role able to address feasible options.",["A diagnosis of low motivation from the absence alone.","A claim that the referral offer proves access.","A rule that resources are irrelevant to participation."],"Practical conditions affect whether intended support can be used."),
("A scoping map does not establish a universally effective model.","A planner treats the coordination review as proof that one model works in every service. What is justified instead?","Contextual comparison of roles and requirements with effectiveness still requiring suitable evidence.",["An identical outcome guarantee everywhere.","Universal eligibility inferred from one example.","A claim that all relevant terminology and populations were included."],"The review's purpose and exclusions constrain transfer."),
("Referral offers differ from access achieved.","Five referral offers are logged while a venue remains inaccessible. What should a report avoid?","Calling the offers demonstrated successful inclusion.",["Recording the offers as an output.","Stating the unresolved access barrier.","Identifying a responsible review role."],"The output does not establish use or progress toward a personal goal."),
("Feedback should not require a public recovery narrative.","A service links continued participation to giving a positive recovery story. What should change?","Remove the pressure and use an appropriate voluntary feedback route.",["Keep the condition to improve evaluation response rates.","Publish stories without separate permissions.","Treat silence as proof of programme success."],"Feedback conditions must respect choice and information boundaries."),
("Unavailability should not be recorded as successful completion.","A fictional support option has no current capacity. What belongs in the plan?","The gap, responsible follow-up and appropriate alternatives without an availability promise.",["A completed-referral label based only on sending details.","A guarantee that capacity will appear later.","Blame directed at the person for needing support."],"An accountable plan represents the actual status and unresolved needs."),
])])
